  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThe first wave of precipitation is moving through northern MI today, \ndropping around a quarter to just under an inch in areas adjacent to \nGrand Traverse Bay so far. Lower amounts east of I-75, with only a \ncouple hundredths near Saginaw Bay. Deep moisture is present, with \nthe 12z KAPX RAOB showing a near daily max 1.49 inch PWAT value. \nWarm rain processes are in action, as the WCD was over 11 kft. \nForcing has remained more moderate, keeping rainfall light to \nmoderate at times, and resulting in totals remaining under an inch. \nThis wet airmass will continue overnight and into Thursday as an \nupper closed low in the desert SW lifts north into the central \nplains and continues to direct the plume of deep tropical \nmoisture towards the Great Lakes region. At the same time, a \nlarger surface cyclone will continue to track eastward over \ncentral CAN. An upper shortwave will help reinforce the \nsouthward progression of the cold front Thursday. Above our\nheads, upper ridging over the SE CONUS will be present \nThursday, as well as broad upper troughing over CAN. These join \nnear the Great Lakes region Thursday, placing northern MI in the\nright entrance region right behind the cold frontal passage, \nwhich will be the afternoon and evening of Thursday. Ahead of \nthe front, a 30 - 40 kt LLJ will stretch from MO through \nsouthern MI. This will likely remain south of M-55, as the \nfrontal boundary will meet with it late morning Thursday. The \nfront near the surface will track across northern MI in the \nafternoon hours. \n\nAll of this is to say that the timing and positioning of forcing \ndoesn't quiet align for repeated rounds of widespread heavy \nrain, or even thunderstorms. Areas of fog will be seen tonight\ninto early Thursday morning as the deep tropical moisture\ncontinues. Widespread light to moderate rain will be seen with \nthat secondary surge of deep moisture early Thursday morning, \nadding another quarter to a half inch of rain over northern \nlower with less over eastern upper. Times of light to moderate \nrain will continue over northern lower into the morning hours as\nthe boundary aloft moves overhead and meets the LLJ to our \nsouth (keeping it south). Midday to the early afternoon hours, \nisolated heavy rain for spots in northern lower will be possible\nwhere the larger scale forcing meets with the lower level \nforcing. There is also less than 100 j/kg of instability near \nthe surface as drier air starts to work its way in and some \npeaks of sunshine could warm the surface up enough. This \ninstability is capped in the mid levels, so it would help \nproduce isolated moderate/heavier rain but not much more. Most \nrain will exit by Thursday evening, with clouds and breezy \nnorthwest winds. Winds will diminish over interior spots with \npartly cloudy skies into Friday morning. Breezy northwest winds \nwill continue around coastal areas. \n\nThe surge of cooler air aloft will occur Friday, and could generate \nsome light lake effect rain showers over eastern upper and along and \nnorth of M-72. This will likely hold in some moisture near the \nsurface for Friday night into Saturday morning, when the center of a \n1025 mb surface high moves directly overhead. Guidance is split on \nif temperatures will reach freezing or not, as fog and even freezing \nfog remains possible into early Saturday morning. Lake surface \ntemperatures in the 50s and 60s remain too warm for freezing \ntemperatures along coastal communities. \n\nA quick hitting weak system will track over CAN and bring brief rain \nchances to eastern upper and near the tip of the mitt late Saturday \ninto Sunday. A cooler CAN airmass will slide over the Great Lakes \nearly next week, with increasing confidence for freezing temps early \nTuesday morning (greater than 60% chance for most interior northern \nlower to dip at or below 32F).",
